    {
        "age": null,
        "album": "",
        "author": null,
        "bookmarked": false,
        "comments": [],
        "date_created": "2026-08-02T13:57:53.235541+00:00",
        "date_dead_since": null,
        "date_published": "2026-08-02T04:00:00+00:00",
        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/0053c2e2-7050-42df-b028-1f115795106c,1785347235165/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C231%2C4428%2C2490%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='A group people in red and white decked out in Canadian swag smile at the camera.' width='620' height='349' title='People from diverse cultural backgrounds take part in Canada Day celebrations in Ottawa.'/><p>For many immigrants, building a new life in Canada can come with an unexpected loss: the gradual fading of their heritage language. Linguists say families should carefully consider whether or not to pass these languages on, as many people later regret not doing so.</p>",
        "id": 6095390,
        "language": "en",
        "link": "https://www.cbc.ca/radio/sunday/migration-language-loss-9.7287384?cmp=rss",
        "manual_status_code": 0,
