        "title": "Languages can disappear through migration. Here's how immigrant families hope to save them",
        "vote": 0
    },
    {
        "age": null,
        "album": "",
        "author": null,
        "bookmarked": false,
        "comments": [],
        "date_created": "2026-08-02T08:57:03.443253+00:00",
        "date_dead_since": null,
        "date_published": "2026-08-02T04:00:00+00:00",
        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/fe161f62-e94c-4153-b0cf-c6f8d9b75cc9,1785531997275/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C353%2C7880%2C4432%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='A man in a hard hat looking at a submarine.' width='620' height='349' title='Prime Minister Mark Carney looks over a 212A class submarine under maintenance as he tours Thyssenkrupp Marine Systems (TKMS), a submarine building facility in Kiel, Germany on Tuesday, Aug. 26, 2025.'/><p>The Canadian government has often spoken about the need for submarines to patrol Canada's vast coastline. But the role and range of of its new submarines go far beyond merely patrolling the three coasts of the country.</p>",
